    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/EGAH8/1JW558P56D/academic-administration-compliance-coordinator": {
      "title": "Academic Administration & Compliance Coordinator",
      "description": "Academic Administration & Compliance Coordinator - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Academic Administration & Compliance Coordinator Work Type Full time Positions 1 Position Published At: 5 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0026332 Category: Professional, HEW 7 Influence and advise on complex policy, legislative and governance matters for informed decision making Gain exposure to a diverse range of programs, professional accreditation requirements, policy matters and university-wide initiatives Varied responsibilities and opportunities to build your knowledge 2 x Full time continuing roles based at Callaghan NSW Be Challenged and Imagine with us Are you someone who enjoys working with complex information, interpreting policy and legislation, and turning detailed requirements into clear, practical advice? The University of Newcastle is seeking an Academic Administration & Compliance Coordinator to play a key role in supporting academic governance, regulatory compliance and the quality of our academic programs. Working across the University, you’ll help ensure our programs and courses meet relevant academic, professional and legislative requirements. You’ll provide advice on policy and compliance, coordinate professional accreditation activities, support governance committees and contribute to reviews that strengthen academic quality and decision-making. This is a varied role offering exposure to a broad range of University operations. If you enjoy solving problems, working with detail and collaborating with diverse stakeholders, this is an opportunity to apply your skills to work that makes a meaningful difference to the student and academic experience. The role You’ll work at the intersection of academic governance, policy and compliance, helping the University maintain high standards across its programs, courses and academic operations. Your responsibilities will include: Academic governance: Coordinate program and course approval processes, provide high-level secretariat support to governance committees, and contribute to effective academic decision-making. Regulatory compliance: Monitor compliance with relevant legislation, regulatory frameworks and higher education standards, including requirements relating to international education. Policy and advice: Interpret complex policies, legislation and regulatory requirements, providing clear, practical advice to academic and professional staff. Program quality and accreditation: Coordinate professional accreditation activities, maintain accreditation records and reporting, monitor conditions and support academic areas to meet their obligations. Review and continuous improvement: Contribute to policy reviews, quality assurance activities and improvements to academic administration and governance processes. Stakeholder engagement: Build effective relationships across Schools, Colleges and professional service areas to coordinate information, resolve issues and progress priorities. About you You’re an analytical and organised professional who can make sense of complex information, identify risks and requirements, and communicate your findings clearly and confidently. You take pride in producing accurate, high-quality work and understand the importance of sound governance, good judgement and attention to detail. You’re comfortable working with legislation, policies and formal documentation, and can balance competing priorities while maintaining strong relationships with stakeholders. To succeed in this role, you’ll bring: Strong analytical skills and the ability to interpret complex documents, synthesise information and identify key issues. Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to draft clear, precise and well-structured advice, policies and reports. Experience in policy, governance, compliance, quality assurance, regulatory affairs or a related field. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to coordinate multiple processes, manage deadlines and maintain accurate records. Sound judgement and the confidence to provide advice, ask questions and follow up on compliance requirements. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work collaboratively with stakeholders across a complex organisation. An interest in law, legislation, business or regulatory frameworks will be valuable. Experience in higher education is welcome, but we’re equally interested in candidates who can bring relevant skills and experience from other regulated or complex environments. These two continuing roles are full time at a HEW 7 Higher Education Worker Level and are located at Callaghan Campus About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision and our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability, the University of Newcastle’s Looking Ahead Strategic Plan 20 30 outlines the University’s commitment to delivering an exceptional student experience and serving our communities. For more information click here – Our University. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: Remuneration is from $107,268 p.a.+ superannuation commensurate with skills and experience Excellent employment conditions in line with University of Newcastle professional staff conditions. Flexible working arrangements (in line with the University’s Flexible Work Policy 35 hour week 17% super and salary packaging Additional University Holiday days, generous personal leave and parental leave, annual leave loading and additional purchased leave scheme Discounts in private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more Wellbeing programs Your next steps Please visit here for a guide on how to apply for a role. If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy before commencing your application. Your application must include: Your resume A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity A statement addressing the Essential Criteria listed in the job description demonstrating your qualifications and experience with examples. To ensure that your application receives full consideration, it's important to structure and address the selection criteria outlined in the application process. This helps us better understand your experience and suitability for the role. We appreciate your attention to this matter and look forward to reviewing your application. Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document. Your Cover Letter and Response to the Essential Criteria should not exceed 4 pages (approximately 2,000 words). Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. We are unable to offer visa sponsorship for this role. For specific position enquiries, please contact John Radvan, Senior Manager Academic Governance and Compliance via email John.Radvan@newcastle.edu.au Having Difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 8 th November 2026 @ 11.59pm AEDST Our assessment process Typically, candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community. This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance—it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you! We are committed to a fair, inclusive, and barrier-free recruitment process. If you require an alternative way to access the Position Description or submit your application, please contact employment@newcastle.edu.au . Reasonable adjustments are available throughout the assessment and interview process to support all applicants to participate fully. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and strongly encourage women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates to apply. #LI-KG1 Related documents HEW07 PD Academic Administration Compliance Coordinator V3 July2026 Published on 21 Oct 2026, 11:29 PM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/NQH6Q/L49U3WITVE/associate-director-collections-and-experience": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Collections and Experience",
      "description": "Associate Director, Collections and Experience - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Associate Director, Collections and Experience Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 8 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0027824 Category: Professional, HEW 10+ Lead the Library's collections and programs portfolio and build advocacy across the sector Provide strategic leadership for the University Library's Collections and Experience portfolio Champion the value and impact of our collections and programs with diverse stakeholders Job no: HRC0027824 Category: HEW 10+ Position: Full Time fixed term (1-year contract) Location: Callaghan Campus Be challenged and imagine with us As Associate Director, Collections and Experience, you will provide strategic leadership across collections, archives and special collections, audience engagement, programming, communications, digital experiences, and emerging technologies. You will lead a highly skilled team responsible for ensuring the University's collections, services, programs, and experiences remain accessible, relevant, and responsive to the evolving needs of students, researchers, staff, and the wider community. This is an opportunity to lead how collections, exhibitions, digital initiatives, student wellbeing programs, and community engagement activities connect people with the Library's resources, expertise, and unique cultural assets in a rapidly evolving higher education environment. In this role you will: Lead the strategic development and delivery of the Library's Collections and Experience portfolio. Provide leadership for collection development, collection stewardship, archives, special collections, and the delivery of engaging and meaningful user experiences. Develop and deliver a unified program of exhibitions, events, student wellbeing initiatives, communications, promotions, social media, and digital engagement activities that showcase the value and impact of the Library. Lead the Library's communications and advocacy activities, translating complex ideas into compelling stories that strengthen awareness, engagement, and support for collections, programs, and services. Oversee the stewardship, preservation, digitisation, interpretation, and promotion of archives, rare books, artefacts, and special collections. About the Library The University Library plays a vital role in supporting students, academics, and researchers, providing a first-class learning environment that fosters collaboration, research excellence, and academic success. With a strong workplace culture and a commitment to innovation, the Library is both a student-facing service and a strategic hub, adapting to the evolving needs of the University community. The Library develops and preserves print and digital collections, supports collaborative learning and research via contemporary and flexible learning facilities and social meeting spaces, and builds the user experience into the design and development of services and programs. Our recognition and support for the changing needs of students, academics, and researchers is exemplified through investment in learning spaces and resources, a client-centred service model that engages staff and clients in ongoing review of service requirements and introducing technology solutions that enhance the client experience. Our libraries are welcoming places where students come to meet, read, study and learn. We operate four libraries across locations at Callaghan, Newcastle City, Gosford, and Sydney. How will you inspire us? Your expertise in collections, audience engagement, and emerging sector trends, combined with knowledge of digital platforms, content management, and risk-informed practice, will ensure the Library continues to innovate, connect students and researchers with collections, and deliver impactful programs and services. Your strong strategic engagement skills will enable you to build partnerships and influence key stakeholders, strengthening the University’s presence within the sector. You will bring a strategic and creative mindset, the ability to navigate complexity and risk, and a leadership approach that fosters innovation, collaboration, and continuous improvement. With extensive experience leading high-performing teams, you will cultivate a positive, productive, and inclusive workplace culture. Equally important is your commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion. As the leading educator of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students in Australia, and the first in NSW for social equity students, the Library is committed to furthering the University’s vision through inclusive and supportive environments for both students and staff. What we offer A highly attractive remuneration package is on offer and a range of flexible salary packaging options is also available. In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. For more information on benefits and conditions, please follow this link . About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision are our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability, the University of Newcastle’s Looking Ahead Strategic Plan 2030 outlines the University’s commitment to delivering an exceptional student experience and serving our communities. Your next steps If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ (or equivalent) to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy from the Related Documents section before commencing your application. Your application must include: Your resume. A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity. A statement addressing the targeted questions below: Explain how your experience, leadership approach, and values align with this role (1 page max) Describe a time when you led a team through a complex or uncertain situation involving competing priorities, risks, and stakeholder expectations. How did you establish a clear direction, communicate key priorities, maintain momentum, manage risk, and achieve positive outcomes? (1 page max) Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document . This document should not exceed 3 pages. Due to the nature of this role, the University may require the preferred candidate to undertake further pre-employment checks. Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. We value diversity and strive to ensure that our recruitment processes are equitable, inclusive, and barrier-free. If you require an alternative pathway to view the Position Description or to submit an application, please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au. We also seek to provide a fair chance to all applicants by providing reasonable adjustments where required during the assessment and interview phases of the recruitment process. Contact Us For specific position enquiries, please contact Lisa Ogle, Acting University Librarian on 0437772451 or via email at lisa.ogle@newcastle.edu.au Having technical difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Please note, we do not accept email applications. Our Assessment Process Typically candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community. This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance—it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Closing date: Monday 2 November 2026 at 11.59pm AEDT The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. The University of Newcastle acknowledges the traditional custodians of the lands within our footprint areas: Awabakal, Darkinjung, Birpai, Worimi, Wonnarua, and Eora Nations. We also pay respect to the wisdom of our Elders past and present. #LI-CP1 Related documents HEW10+ Associate Director Collections and Experience (fixed term) Published on 19 Oct 2026, 2:50 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
    }
  },
